/** \addtogroup area_stack */
/** \defgroup theory_stack Theory of stacks
 \ingroup area_stack

 \details The headers of `include/ADS/stack` realize the STACK of section
 4.3.1 of the book, i.e., with a vector of a given capacity (section 4.3.2),
 on the operations of any SEQUENCE, and, as several stacks in one vector,
 following exercise 4.7; beside them are the algorithms that use a stack
 through its operations alone, among which the evaluation of the arithmetic
 expressions in postfix notation and the translation of the infix ones. This
 page collects the definitions and the notation that the headers share (see
 \ref theory_stack_notation), the realizations with the costs of their
 operations (see \ref theory_stack_repr), and, for the algorithms, the
 property on which each is correct and the argument behind its bound (see
 \ref theory_stack_expr and \ref theory_stack_algo). The pseudocode of each
 operation and of each algorithm is on the page of its header, in the comment
 of its function, together with the section of the book, or the paper, that
 it follows.

 \tableofcontents

 \section theory_stack_notation Definitions and notation

 A <em>stack</em> is a sequence of items to which items are added, and from
 which they are removed, at one end only, the <em>top</em>: the last item
 added is the first removed (LIFO, last in first out). The STACK of the book
 has `push()`, which adds an item on top, `pop()`, which removes the item on
 top and returns it, `top()`, which reads it, and `empty()`; its axioms are
 \f$ \mathrm{top}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = v \f$ and
 \f$ \mathrm{pop}( \mathrm{push}( S , v ) ) = S \f$, with `top()` and
 `pop()` undefined on the empty stack (see \ref theory_ADS_adt, and the
 concept `Stack` of `ADT.h`). A stack is what a computation needs when it
 has to come back to a point in the reverse order in which it left it: the
 calls of a program, whose frames make the stack of the calls, the
 parentheses of an expression, the moves of a backtracking.

 <table>
 <caption>The symbols of the headers of the area</caption>
 <tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ n \f$</td><td>the number of items of a stack, or of tokens
     of an expression</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ c \f$</td><td>the capacity of a stack on a vector</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ k \f$</td><td>the number of stacks that share one vector, or
     a key of <tt>removeGreater()</tt></td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ S \f$</td><td>a stack, or the stack of the operators of a
     translation</td></tr>
 <tr><td>\f$ s \mathbin{\mathrm{op}} t \f$</td><td>a binary operation, with
     \f$ s \f$ its left operand and \f$ t \f$ its right one</td></tr>
 </table>

 \section theory_stack_repr Realizations and their costs

 <table>
 <caption>The realizations of the STACK and the cost of their operations
          (sections 4.3.1 and 4.3.2 of the book, exercise 4.7)</caption>
 <tr><th>Realization</th><th>Representation</th><th>Space</th>
     <th><tt>push()</tt>, <tt>pop()</tt>, <tt>top()</tt></th>
     <th>Capacity</th></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>ArrayStack</tt></td><td>a vector, the top in the place
     \f$ n - 1 \f$</td><td>\f$ \Theta( c ) \f$</td><td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ c \f$, fixed</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>SequenceStack</tt></td><td>a SEQUENCE, the top its first
     item</td><td>that of the sequence</td><td>those of
     <tt>insert()</tt>, <tt>remove()</tt> and <tt>read()</tt> at the head,
     \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ on a list</td><td>none</td></tr>
 <tr><td><tt>MultiStack</tt></td><td>\f$ k \f$ stacks of linked cells in one
     vector, with a free list</td><td>\f$ \Theta( n + k ) \f$</td>
     <td>\f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized</td><td>none, the vector
     doubles</td></tr>
 </table>

 On a vector the \f$ n \f$ items are in the places \f$ 0 , \ldots , n - 1 \f$
 and the top in the place \f$ n - 1 \f$, so that `push()` writes the place
 \f$ n \f$ and `pop()` reads the place \f$ n - 1 \f$, each in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$,
 and the precondition of `push()` is that the stack is not full. A
 SEQUENCE simulates the stack with its top as the first item:
 \f[
   \mathrm{top}() = \mathrm{read}( \mathrm{head}() ) , \qquad
   \mathrm{pop}() = \mathrm{remove}( \mathrm{head}() ) , \qquad
   \mathrm{push}( v ) = \mathrm{insert}( \mathrm{head}() , v ) , \tag{1}
 \f]
 which costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ on any list with pointers, even a singly linked
 one, while on a vector the top would rather be the last item, since an
 insertion at the head moves all the items.

 Two stacks in one vector of \f$ c \f$ places (exercise 4.7) grow from its
 two ends toward each other, and neither is full while the vector has a free
 place. `MultiStack` extends the idea to \f$ k \f$ stacks: each item is a
 cell of a single vector linked to the cell below it in its stack, and the
 free cells form one more list. A `push()` takes the first free cell, a
 `pop()` gives its cell back, each in \f$ O( 1 ) \f$; when no cell is free
 the vector doubles, which costs \f$ O( 1 ) \f$ amortized per operation (see
 \ref theory_sequence_amortized). The space is that of the \f$ n \f$ items
 of all the stacks, plus the \f$ k \f$ tops, against the \f$ k c \f$ places
 of \f$ k \f$ vectors each large enough for the largest stack.

 \section theory_stack_expr Arithmetic expressions

 An expression in <em>postfix</em> notation (the reverse Polish notation of
 Łukasiewicz) writes each operator after its two operands, e.g.,
 `5 9 8 + 4 6 * * 7 + *` for \f$ 5 \cdot ( ( 9 + 8 ) \cdot ( 4 \cdot 6 ) + 7 )
 \f$; it needs no parentheses and no precedences, since the order of the
 tokens is that of the evaluation. Formally, an operand is a postfix
 expression, and so is \f$ E_1 \, E_2 \, \mathrm{op} \f$ when \f$ E_1 \f$ and
 \f$ E_2 \f$ are.

 <em>Lemma 1 (the evaluation of a postfix expression).</em> Let a scan of the
 tokens push each operand, and pop, for each operator, the right operand
 \f$ t \f$ and then the left one \f$ s \f$, and push
 \f$ s \mathbin{\mathrm{op}} t \f$. Then the scan of a postfix expression
 \f$ E \f$ that starts with a stack \f$ S \f$ ends with \f$ S \f$ and the
 value of \f$ E \f$ on top of it; hence the value of a whole expression is
 the one item left, after \f$ n \f$ steps of \f$ O( 1 ) \f$.

 Indeed, by induction on the structure of \f$ E \f$: an operand is pushed;
 for \f$ E = E_1 \, E_2 \, \mathrm{op} \f$, the scan of \f$ E_1 \f$ leaves
 its value \f$ s \f$ on \f$ S \f$, that of \f$ E_2 \f$ leaves its value
 \f$ t \f$ on \f$ S \f$ and \f$ s \f$, and the operator replaces the two with
 \f$ s \mathbin{\mathrm{op}} t \f$. A token sequence that is not well formed
 shows as an operator that finds fewer than two items, or as more than one
 item left at the end, which are the preconditions of `evalPostfix()`.

 A <em>fully parenthesized</em> infix expression has a pair of parentheses
 around each operation, e.g., `( 5 * ( ( ( 9 + 8 ) * ( 4 * 6 ) ) + 7 ) )`;
 its postfix form is obtained by moving each operator to the closed
 parenthesis of its pair, i.e., by writing the operands as they come,
 pushing the operators and popping one at each closed parenthesis, since the
 innermost pair open is the last one opened. With the usual precedences
 instead (\f$ \cdot \f$ and \f$ / \f$ before \f$ + \f$ and \f$ - \f$) and
 the associativity to the left, the shunting-yard algorithm of Dijkstra
 (`toPostfix()`) keeps on the stack the operators whose right operand is
 not complete yet: an operator \f$ \mathrm{op} \f$ first pops to the output,
 down to an open parenthesis, every operator of precedence not lower than
 its own, whose right operand ends where \f$ \mathrm{op} \f$ begins; a closed
 parenthesis pops them down to the open one. The operators on the stack have
 therefore precedences that increase strictly from the bottom up between
 two parentheses, each token is pushed and popped at most once, and the
 translation costs \f$ O( n ) \f$ (Samelson and Bauer, Dijkstra).

 \section theory_stack_algo Algorithms on a stack

 The functions of `StackAlgorithms.h` act on a stack through `push()`,
 `pop()`, `top()` and `empty()` alone, so that they run on every
 realization. A string is a palindrome if and only if the characters pushed
 in order and popped come out as the string read from its start, since the
 stack reverses the order; hence \f$ O( n ) \f$ time. Removing the items
 larger than a key \f$ k \f$ while keeping the order of the others takes an
 auxiliary stack, which reverses them once when they are moved there and once
 more when they come back, i.e., \f$ O( n ) \f$ time and space.

 The recursion supplies a second stack, that of the calls. To visit the
 items from the bottom to the top and leave the stack as it was,
 `forEachBottomUp()` pops the top, visits the rest by a recursive call,
 visits the top and pushes it back: the call stack holds the \f$ n \f$ items
 popped, in \f$ O( n ) \f$ time and space. In the same way `sortStack()` sorts
 a stack with no other structure: a pass pops two items, pushes back the
 larger, makes a recursive pass on the rest and pushes the smaller on top.

 <em>Lemma 2 (the sort of a stack by passes).</em> A pass carries the
 largest item of the stack to its bottom and leaves the others in their
 order, but for the swaps of the pairs met along the way; hence after the
 pass \f$ i \f$ the \f$ i \f$ largest items are at the bottom in order, and
 \f$ n \f$ passes sort the stack, the least item on top, with
 \f$ O( n^2 ) \f$ operations.

 Indeed, by induction on the size, the larger of the two items popped is
 pushed back and the recursive pass carries the largest of the rest, which
 is at least as large, to the bottom, while the smaller of the two is pushed
 last, one place above where it was; an item of the bottom \f$ i - 1 \f$,
 already larger than all the others, is never moved above them. Each pass
 costs \f$ O( n ) \f$ and makes \f$ O( n ) \f$ recursive calls, and the
 small items rise by one place per pass, as in the bubble sort, of which
 this is the version on a stack (see \ref theory_sequence_sort).

